        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most walls do not need a separate primer at all, and the walls that do usually need a specific one. A general-purpose water-based primer will not stop a nicotine stain, and a shellac stain-killer is overkill on new drywall. Match the primer to the problem and the topcoat takes care of itself.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="when-you-need-primer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. When You Actually Need Primer
          </h2>

          <p>
            The honest answer is: less often than the paint aisle suggests, and more specifically than most people realize. Primer does three jobs. It seals porous surfaces so the topcoat dries evenly, it gives paint something to grip on smooth or difficult surfaces, and it blocks stains and odors that would otherwise bleed through. If none of those problems exist, you probably do not need a separate primer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repainting a sound, clean wall in a similar color is the classic case where you can skip it. A quality <a href="/best-interior-paint/">interior paint</a> sold as paint-and-primer is simply a high-solids paint that covers well; it will handle a same-sheen repaint over existing latex without a dedicated primer coat. The same goes for most <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">ceiling paints</a> going over an existing flat white ceiling that has no stains.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do need primer in six situations: bare or new drywall, fresh patches and <a href="/best-spackle/">spackle</a> repairs, stains of any kind that have to be hidden, glossy or non-porous surfaces, bare wood (especially knotty pine, cedar and redwood), and big color changes where going dark to light would otherwise take three or four coats of expensive paint. Surfaces that smell of smoke, pets or fire damage also need a primer that seals odor, and paint-and-primer products do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spot-priming deserves a mention because it is where most DIY repaints go wrong. A patch of joint compound or spackle absorbs paint differently from the painted wall around it, and even after two coats it shows as a dull, flat blotch in raking light — a defect called flashing. A quick coat of primer over every patch before painting prevents it. It is the single cheapest improvement to most repaint jobs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sound, clean, same color, no stains? Skip primer. Anything else? Match the primer to the problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Paint-and-primer products are good paint, not a replacement for a stain-blocking or bonding primer. They save a coat on easy repaints and do nothing for water rings, glossy trim or raw drywall.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Water-Based vs Oil-Based vs Shellac
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every primer on the shelf is one of three chemistries, and the choice between them matters more than the brand.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best at</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Odor / cleanup</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical recoat</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based (acrylic)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">New drywall, general priming, exterior trim, low-odor stain blocking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild / soap and water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 hour</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-based (alkyd)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy stains, tannin bleed, chalky and porous surfaces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong / mineral spirits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shellac</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Severe stains, knots, smoke and fire damage, odor sealing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very strong / denatured alcohol</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 45 minutes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bonding (acrylic-urethane)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Glossy tile, laminate, PVC, glass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild / soap and water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 hours, days to cure</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Water-based primers are the default for most work. They are low odor, dry fast, clean up with water and stay flexible, which makes them the right choice for drywall and most exterior trim. Their weakness is stains. Many common stains, including water marks, tannin and nicotine, are water-soluble, and a water-based primer can re-dissolve them and carry them to the surface. Premium water-based stain blockers like KILZ Restoration have narrowed that gap considerably, but they have not closed it entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil-based primers seal porous and chalky surfaces better and stop most stains because the solvent does not dissolve water-soluble contaminants. They are slower, smell strongly and clean up with mineral spirits, and on exterior surfaces they can become brittle over time, so an exterior-rated formula matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shellac is in a category of its own. It dries in minutes, blocks almost everything, and seals odors — but it has strong alcohol fumes, is not designed for whole exterior surfaces, and is overkill for ordinary walls. Keep a quart for problem spots rather than priming whole rooms with it.
          </p>
        </div>

