        <div id="stain-blocking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Stain Blocking: Water, Smoke and Knots
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stains are the main reason people buy primer, and the main reason primer jobs fail. The rule is simple: fix the cause first, let the surface dry, and then choose a primer strong enough for the stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water stains on ceilings are the most common. Confirm the leak has been fixed, let the drywall dry for several days, and check it is firm rather than soft; soft drywall needs replacing, not priming. A light brown ring will usually stay hidden under a premium water-based stain blocker, while a dark ring with a tide-mark edge is a job for shellac or oil. Prime a few inches beyond the visible edge, because the stain usually extends further than you can see. If you are then painting the whole ceiling, a flat ceiling paint over the primer gives the most uniform finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smoke and nicotine leave a yellow-brown film that is partly greasy. Wash walls first with a degreasing cleaner, rinse and let dry, otherwise no primer bonds well. Light nicotine staining responds to KILZ Restoration; heavy smoke damage and soot after a fire need B-I-N or an oil-based stain killer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Knots in pine and other softwoods bleed resin and tannin through latex paint, sometimes months after painting. Oil and acrylic primers often slow this down without stopping it. Shellac is the traditional fix: spot-prime each knot with B-I-N, let it dry, then prime the whole board. Cedar and redwood bleed tannin across the entire surface, so on exterior siding use an oil-based primer rated for tannin blocking, such as Cover Stain.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">STAIN-KILLER DRY TIMES FROM MANUFACTURER DATA</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">B-I-N dry to touch</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">45 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">B-I-N recoat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">KILZ Restoration recoat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">35 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cover Stain dry to touch</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Whatever you use, test it. Prime the stain, wait for the recoat time, and put a coat of the actual finish paint over a small area. If the stain ghosts through, add a second coat of primer before painting the rest of the room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bonding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Bonding Primers for Gloss, Tile and Laminate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint does not peel from glossy surfaces because the paint is poor; it peels because it never had anything to grip. Glossy enamel trim, ceramic tile, laminate cabinets, PVC and fiberglass are all smooth enough that latex paint sits on top rather than bonding, and a fingernail or a piece of tape will lift it later.
          </p>

          <p>
            A bonding primer solves that with a resin chemistry designed to adhere to hard, non-porous surfaces. INSL-X Stix is the specialist, using an acrylic-urethane resin that bonds to glazed tile, PVC and glass. Shellac bonds well to most glossy surfaces too, and several multi-purpose primers, including Bulls Eye 1-2-3 and Cover Stain, are rated for gloss adhesion, which is usually enough for painted trim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preparation matters as much as the product. Clean the surface with a degreaser to remove kitchen grease, hand oils and soap film, rinse thoroughly, and scuff-sand with 220-grit to dull the sheen. Even bonding primers that say sanding is optional perform better on a scuffed surface. Wipe off dust with a damp cloth before priming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cure time is the hidden requirement. Bonding primers take days, not hours, to reach full adhesion. Stix needs three to four days to reach maximum scrape resistance, which is why a scratch test the next morning can make it seem like the primer failed. Build that into your schedule, particularly for cabinet doors that will be handled constantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cabinets and trim, application quality shows more than on walls. A fine-finish <a href="/best-paint-brushes/">paint brush</a> or a sprayer gives the smoothest result, and a bonding primer that levels well reduces the sanding needed between coats.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drywall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Priming New and Damaged Drywall
          </h2>

          <p>
            New drywall has two very different surfaces: the paper face and the joint compound over seams and screws. They absorb paint at different rates, so without a primer the seams show through the finished wall as dull stripes, especially in raking light from a window. A primer evens out the porosity so the topcoat dries uniformly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For large areas of new drywall, an inexpensive PVA drywall primer is designed exactly for this job and is what most contractors use. It seals the surface well but does not block stains and has limited adhesion on anything other than drywall. A general-purpose primer like KILZ 2 or Bulls Eye 1-2-3 costs a little more and also handles trim, patches and minor marks, which is useful when a basement or addition includes all of those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Damaged drywall is a different problem. When wallpaper removal tears the face paper, the brown layer underneath absorbs water from paint or compound and bubbles. A normal water-based primer makes it worse. The fix is Zinsser Gardz or a shellac primer, which seals and hardens the paper before any water-based product touches it. Gardz is also the right first coat over wallpaper paste residue, which otherwise causes latex paint to crack.
          </p>

          <p>
            After sealing, skim-coat any rough areas, sand smooth, and then prime again with a pigmented primer so the new compound does not flash through the paint. For small holes and dents, a lightweight <a href="/best-spackle/">spackle</a> and a spot of primer is enough; for torn paper over a whole wall, budget for the full sealer, skim coat and primer sequence.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DRYWALL RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Torn paper gets a sealer before any water touches it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Gardz or shellac first, joint compound second, pigmented primer third. Reverse that order and the bubbles come back with every coat.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="odor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Odor Blocking After Smoke, Pets and Fire
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint does not cover odors; it adds a fresh-paint smell that fades within weeks and leaves the original odor behind. A primer that seals odor works by forming a film that traps odor molecules in the surface, and only certain primers do this well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shellac is the benchmark. B-I-N is the product restoration contractors use after house fires, and it is the most reliable choice for severe smoke odor. Oil-based stain killers such as KILZ Original also seal smoke, pet and food odors, and KILZ Restoration is the water-based option rated for smoke and pet odor, which makes it the easiest to use in a home you are living in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Odor sealing depends on complete coverage. The smell comes from every surface in the room, not only the walls you can see, so prime the ceiling, closet interiors, the backs of doors and the trim. In a house with heavy smoking, ceilings often hold more residue than walls because smoke rises. Missing a closet or a ceiling is the most common reason the smell comes back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pet urine needs extra steps. It soaks into subfloors and the bottom of drywall, so clean with an enzyme cleaner, let everything dry completely, then seal the affected subfloor and the bottom of the walls with shellac or an oil-based primer before new flooring goes down. Sealing over damp or contaminated material simply traps the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilation still matters during the work. Shellac and oil primers release strong fumes as they dry, so open windows, use a box fan blowing out, and wear an organic vapor respirator in small rooms. The fumes clear quickly once the film has dried; the sealed odor does not come back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tinting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Tinting Primer for Big Color Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A big color change is where primer can save the most money. Going from a dark red or navy to a pale color over the old paint can take three or four coats of finish paint, while one coat of primer followed by two coats of paint usually does the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tinting the primer toward the finish color makes that work even better. Most water-based and oil-based primers, including Bulls Eye 1-2-3, can be tinted at the paint counter. The standard advice for light-to-dark changes is to tint primer to about half the strength of the topcoat, or to use a gray primer under deep reds, blues and greens. Deep colors often have poor hiding power on their own, and a white primer under them can take extra coats to cover completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            For dark-to-light changes, a high-hiding white primer is usually the right choice. Primers are designed to hide, and a white primer covers a dark wall more effectively than a light paint. On a very dark wall, two coats of primer before the finish paint can still be cheaper than extra coats of premium paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a limit to how much you can tint. Primers can take only a limited amount of colorant before performance drops, so the store will usually not tint primer to the full depth of a deep topcoat. That is normal and is why gray primer exists.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you are changing colors, keep edges sharp. Use a good-quality painter's tape at ceilings and trim and remove it while the final coat is still slightly wet to avoid peeling dried paint. Our guide to the best painter's tape explains which tapes work on fresh primer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="exterior" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Exterior Priming
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior primers face a harder job: sun, rain, temperature swings and wood that expands and contracts throughout the year. The primer must stay flexible, resist mildew and, on many woods, block tannin bleed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bare wood is the most important thing to prime outside. New siding, replacement trim and areas scraped back to bare wood all need primer before painting, and exposed wood should be primed promptly because sun-grayed wood loses adhesion. For cedar and redwood, use a primer rated for tannin blocking, such as Cover Stain. For pine and most other trim, an exterior-rated water-based primer like Bulls Eye 1-2-3 or KILZ 2 works well and stays flexible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Previously painted exterior surfaces that are sound usually need only spot priming. Scrape loose paint, sand the edges smooth, and prime bare spots. If the old paint is chalky — it leaves a white powder on your hand — wash it, and use a primer rated for chalky surfaces, since paint applied directly over chalk will not bond reliably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the temperature guidance on the can. Most primers have a minimum surface and air temperature and need a dry period after application. Avoid priming in direct afternoon sun or when dew is expected before the primer dries, because both can affect adhesion and finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seal gaps before painting. Caulk joints in trim after priming, because caulk bonds better to primed wood than to bare wood. Our guide to <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk</a> covers which products accept paint and stay flexible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Apply Primer So It Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Primer is usually applied the same way as paint, but because it is the base coat, preparation and application mistakes show up in the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with a clean, dull and dry surface. Wash grease and grime off walls, especially in kitchens and bathrooms, and let everything dry. Sand glossy surfaces and fill holes, then remove dust. Primer bonds only to what is underneath it; if that is dust or grease, the whole paint job will fail with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose the right tool. Rollers are fast on walls and ceilings, and brushes are best for cutting in and trim. A good <a href="/best-paint-brushes/">brush</a> matters more with shellac and oil primers, which dry quickly and show brush marks. For whole rooms, cabinets or exterior siding, an airless <a href="/best-paint-sprayer/">paint sprayer</a> lays down primer quickly and evenly; thin only as the manufacturer allows, and always back-roll porous surfaces for better penetration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use tape where you need sharp lines, and apply it before priming rather than after. A quality <a href="/best-painters-tape/">painter's tape</a> will not lift fresh primer when removed. With fast-drying shellac, remove the tape as soon as the primer is dry to the touch.
          </p>

          <p>
            One coat of primer is usually enough on drywall and sound surfaces. Use two coats over severe stains, when changing from very dark to very light, or over very porous surfaces. After priming, lightly sand with fine-grit sandpaper to remove raised grain or dust, wipe clean, and apply the topcoat once the recoat time has passed. Some primers also list a maximum time before topcoating, after which adhesion suffers, so do not leave primed walls unpainted for months.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAND BETWEEN</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A quick pass with 220-grit after priming is the difference between good and great.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Primer raises wood grain and holds dust. Two minutes of light sanding before the topcoat removes both and gives trim and cabinets a much smoother finish.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Priming stains before fixing the cause</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A water stain returns if the leak is not repaired and the drywall has not dried. Primer seals a stain; it does not stop fresh water from coming through. Fix the plumbing or roof, wait several days, confirm the surface is firm and dry, and only then prime it with a proper stain-blocking product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a water-based primer on torn drywall paper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Exposed drywall paper absorbs water and bubbles, and a latex primer adds more water. The bubbles often get worse with every coat. Seal torn paper with Gardz or shellac first, let it dry, skim-coat if needed, then use a pigmented primer before painting the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing a bonding primer too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bonding primers take days to reach full adhesion. Scratch Stix or a similar primer the morning after applying it and it may lift, making it seem like it failed. Follow the cure time on the data sheet and avoid heavy use or tape on the surface until it has cured.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Priming only the walls after smoke damage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Smoke odor comes from every surface in the room, and ceilings often hold the most residue. Priming only walls leaves the smell in the ceiling, closets and trim. Clean everything, then seal all surfaces with an odor-blocking primer for a lasting result.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on paint-and-primer for problem surfaces</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint-and-primer products are good paints that hide well on easy repaints. They are not stain blockers, bonding primers or drywall sealers. Use a dedicated primer over stains, gloss, bare wood and new drywall, then use the paint you prefer as the topcoat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need primer if I use paint and primer in one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for repainting a sound, clean surface in a similar color, where paint-and-primer products work well. You still need a separate primer over new drywall, patches, stains, glossy surfaces, bare wood and major color changes. Paint-and-primer products do not block stains or improve adhesion the way dedicated primers do, and they cost more per coat than primer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best primer for water stains on a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fix the leak and let the ceiling dry first. For light stains, a premium water-based stain blocker such as KILZ Restoration works well. For dark rings or stains that have already bled through paint, use a shellac primer like Zinsser B-I-N or an oil-based stain blocker. Prime beyond the stain edge, then paint the ceiling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oil-based primer better than water-based?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in general. Oil-based primers are better at blocking tough stains, tannin bleed and sealing chalky surfaces. Water-based primers are better for new drywall, general use and low-odor work, and they stay flexible outside. Premium water-based stain blockers now handle many jobs that once needed oil. Choose based on the problem rather than the chemistry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint latex over oil-based primer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Most oil-based primers, including KILZ Original and Zinsser Cover Stain, can be topcoated with latex or oil paint once dry. Shellac primers also accept latex and oil topcoats. What you should avoid is applying latex directly over old oil-based gloss paint without priming, because the latex usually cannot bond to it and will peel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should primer dry before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the type. Shellac primers such as B-I-N can often be recoated in about 45 minutes, while most water-based and oil-based primers need around one to two hours. Bonding primers can be topcoated in a few hours but need several days to fully cure. Humidity and cold slow drying, so check the can.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of primer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One coat is enough for most drywall, patches and sound painted surfaces. Use two coats over severe stains, strong odors, very porous surfaces and major dark-to-light color changes. If a stain shows through after one coat and a test patch of paint, add another coat of primer before painting the rest of the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use interior primer outside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is rated for exterior use. Primers like Bulls Eye 1-2-3, KILZ 2 and Cover Stain are rated for interior and exterior use, while KILZ Restoration and KILZ Original are interior products. Shellac primers such as B-I-N are for interior and exterior spot priming. Exterior-rated primers stay more flexible and resist mildew better.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

